# Tech-support scam pop-up (2010s onwards). A web page pretends the PC is locked, then a "support" caller talks the victim into installing a remote-access program.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee keeps an office stocked, does one order safely, then meets the pop-up while looking up a courier price.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | A tech-support scam starts with a web page that looks like a Windows warning and gives a phone number. Nothing is wrong with the computer. The harm starts when the victim calls and lets "support" connect with a remote-access program, then pays to have problems fixed that never existed.
  | These pop-ups reach any office worker who browses. The victim here is invented: scam pop-ups were seen by people in every kind of job.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, office manager at Northwind Traders. You keep the office running: toner, paper, couriers. You buy supplies with the company card.
  | It is mid-afternoon. You have done 5 of today's 7 supplier jobs. Two to go: the printer toner, then a courier price for Marcus.
  | The company card details are in a text file on the desktop. That is a bad habit, but it keeps this training quick.
quota 5 7 Supplier jobs done today

# Dana's desk: the details she copies from (a soft flag: careless, but only here to keep the training simple).
file Desktop/company-card.txt
  at 930 200
  | Northwind company card (training copy)
  | Name on card: Dana Okafor
  | Card number: 4000 0012 3456 7899
  | Security code: 318

# The safe job: reorder toner from the usual supplier.
page toner-done supplies-direct.example/orders/added
  title Added to your order
  brand Supplies Direct
  heading Added to your order
  | 2 x black toner cartridge T-410 will be delivered to Northwind Traders, floor 2, on Thursday.
page toner-order supplies-direct.example/orders/toner
  title Reorder: black toner
  brand Supplies Direct
  heading Reorder: black toner
  | Your usual cartridge is ready to reorder.
  section Your order
  row Item = Black toner cartridge T-410
  row Quantity = 2
  row Price = 86.00
  row Deliver to = Northwind Traders, floor 2
  button Add to order -> toner-done
mail mail-toner
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject Printer toner is nearly out
  link toner-order
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | The floor 2 printer is down to its last pages. Could you reorder two cartridges from Supplies Direct? The link goes to our usual order.
  |
  | Priya
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Printer toner is nearly out

step Job 6 of 7: toner
  look the address bar: the supplier's own address, with its padlock
  when visit toner-order
  move Check the order, then click “Add to order”.
  | A new job from Priya: toner for the floor 2 printer. This one is ordinary. Do it the normal way, so you know what normal looks like.
  | The link goes to supplies-direct.example, the supplier you always use. The page is quiet: an order, a price, one button. No alarms, no phone numbers.
open mail-toner
open toner-order

step Toner ordered
  look the order page and the counter in the guide: one job done
  when choose toner-order any
  move Open Mail, open Marcus's new message and click the link under it.
  | The page was quiet: the supplier's own address, an order, a price and one button. That is what a normal page looks like. Done. One job to go.
  | Marcus has just sent you the next one.
open toner-order
choose toner-order 1
quota +1

# The lure: a fake security alert from a price-comparison site. Its buttons lead on to the scam.
page ts-scan pc-support-alert.invalid/scan
  title Threat scan
  insecure
  heading 4 threats found
  | Scan finished. Threats were found on this computer.
  section Scan results
  columns Threat | Location | Risk
  cells Trojan.Banker | C:\Windows\System32 | Critical
  cells Password stealer | Browser data | Critical
  cells Remote hacker | Network | High
  cells Spyware | Documents | High
  button Call support
  button Close
page locked pc-support-alert.invalid/security
  title Security alert
  insecure
  heading Your computer has been locked
  | Error code 0x80073b01. Your files, passwords and banking details are at risk.
  | Do not restart your computer. Call ClearLine Support now on +1 555 0100 (toll free).
  section Security check
  tone bad
  row Threat = Trojan.Banker
  row Your IP = 203.0.113.41
  row Status = Blocked until you call
  button Call support -> ts-scan
  button Close
page courier-rates parcel-compare.example/rates
  title Courier prices
  brand Parcel Compare
  heading Compare courier prices
  | Enter your parcel and see today's prices from six couriers.
  section Your parcel
  row From = Northwind Traders, Leeds
  row To = Contoso Foods, Bristol
  row Weight = 20 kg
  button Show prices -> locked
mail mail-courier
  from Marcus Lee <marcus.lee@northwind.example>
  subject Courier price for the Contoso parcel
  link courier-rates
  | Dana,
  |
  | Could you check what a courier would charge for the 20 kg parcel to Contoso? I use this comparison site. I only need the cheapest price.
  |
  | Marcus
notify mail Marcus Lee
  | Courier price for the Contoso parcel

step Marcus wants a courier price
  look the price comparison page that Marcus's link opened
  when visit courier-rates
  move Click “Show prices”.
  | Marcus uses this price-comparison site, so the page looks like the one he meant. The trouble will come from the page itself.
open courier-rates

step A page says the PC is locked
  look the page with the phone number and the "Not secure" address
  when choose courier-rates 1
  move Click “Call support” on the page.
  | Instead of prices, the page now claims the computer is locked and gives a "support" number. A bad ad redirected it. Nothing is wrong with the computer.
  | It is styled like a Windows warning, but it is only a web page. Flag anything that looks wrong.
choose courier-rates 1

step A scan that finds everything
  look the scan results and the Call support button
  when choose locked 1
  move Click “Call support” on the scan page.
  | The page runs a scan that looks real. It lists four threats, but a web page cannot look inside a computer.
  | The only button that works says Call support.
choose locked 1

# The fake blue screen. It fills the screen, and its Close button does nothing.
view ts-block screen
  title Your computer has been blocked
  bg #0a4fa8
  fg #ffffff
  accent #ffffff
  fullscreen
  heading :( Your computer has been blocked
  | Stop code: 0x80073b01. Hackers may be taking your passwords. Do not restart or turn off your computer.
  | Call ClearLine Support now on +1 555 0100. A technician will fix this for you.
  section
  timer Calling support in = 60
  button Call support now -> done primary
  button Close -> none

step The fake blue screen
  look the blue screen with the number and the countdown
  when choose ts-scan 1
  move Click “Call support now”. You dial the number: it goes to the scammers.
  | Next comes a full-screen blue screen. The victim cannot find a way out and rings the number, which goes to the scammers.
  | Its Close button does nothing. A victim who wants out has to end the browser in {Task Manager|Activity Monitor}.
choose ts-scan 1
show ts-block

# The remote-access program the "technician" asks the caller to install. It is a real, signed tool: the trick is the caller.
view ts-link wizard
  title ClearLine Remote
  app ClearLine Remote
  page Let ClearLine Support see your screen?
  icon question
  | Agent "Kevin" asks to see your screen. Read the session code 482 913 to the agent.
  section Session
  row Session code = 482 913
  row Agent = Kevin (ClearLine Support)
  button Allow -> next primary
  button Decline -> close
  page Allow control of this computer?
  icon warning
  | Kevin asks to use your keyboard and mouse.
  section Recommended options
  check [x] Allow control of mouse and keyboard
  check [x] Start ClearLine Remote with Windows (unattended access)
  check [x] Blank my screen while connected
  button Allow -> next primary
  button Decline -> close
  page Remote session active
  icon bug
  | Kevin can now see and control this computer.
  section What happens next
  row Remote control = On
  row Your screen = Blanked
  row Start with Windows = Yes
  tone bad
  button End session -> close

program ts-remote ClearLineRemote-Setup
  publisher ClearLine Software Ltd
  signed yes
  prompt open-warning
  prompt elevation
  description ClearLine Remote setup
  view ts-link

# The payment pages Kevin opens in the browser once he has control.
page clearline-plan pay.clearline-support.invalid/plan
  title Payment received
  brand ClearLine Support
  heading Thank you, Dana
  | Your ClearLine Protection Plan is active. Kevin will keep your computer protected.
  section Receipt
  row Plan = ClearLine Protection Plan, 1 year
  row Amount = 299.00 USD
  row Card = ending 7899
page clearline-pay pay.clearline-support.invalid/card
  title ClearLine Support: card payment
  brand ClearLine Support
  heading Pay for your Protection Plan
  | Amount due: 299.00 USD. Enter the card details Kevin asked you to use.
  field Name on card = Dana Okafor
  field Card number = 4000 0012 3456 7899
  field secret Security code = 318
  button Pay 299.00 USD -> clearline-plan

step The caller asks for a program
  look the file ClearLineRemote-Setup in Downloads, and the download notice
  when done ts-block
  move Open your Downloads folder and double-click “ClearLineRemote-Setup”.
  | On the phone, "Kevin" sounds calm and friendly. He sends you to a site to download a "support tool". It is saved in Downloads.
  | He says he can only fix the computer from inside it, and that you must open the file now.
file Downloads/ClearLineRemote-Setup.exe
  program ts-remote
  size 3145728
notify browser Download complete
  | ClearLineRemote-Setup.exe was saved to Downloads.

step The warning Kevin says to ignore
  look {Publisher “ClearLine Software Ltd”, Type “Application”|the “is a Windows program” sheet}
  when prompt ts-remote
  cue dialog-btn:run
  move Click {Run|Show what it does}, as Kevin says.
  | {Windows|macOS} asks before a downloaded program runs. Kevin says the warning is normal and tells you to go ahead.
open Downloads/ClearLineRemote-Setup.exe

step Yes, yes, yes
  look ClearLine Remote asking to share the screen
  when run ts-remote
  move Click Allow on both pages of the ClearLine Remote window.
  | {Windows|macOS} asks twice: to run the file, and to let it make changes. Kevin tells you to say yes every time.
  | Then the program asks to share your screen. Kevin says it only needs to see the problem.
answer run
answer run

step Kevin takes over the computer
  look the remote window, then {Task Manager|Activity Monitor} showing ClearLineRemote.exe
  when page ts-link 3
  move Check {Task Manager|Activity Monitor} and the start-up list (the chips below). Then copy the details from company-card.txt on the desktop: click a box, press any keys to type, then click “Pay 299.00 USD”.
  | The program asks for the screen, then for control, with start-up access already ticked. Kevin can now use the computer, and it starts every time the computer does.
  | Kevin opens {Event Viewer|Console} and calls its normal warnings "proof" of an attack. Then he says the fix costs 299.00 USD, and the payment page opens in your browser.
  | While he works, the program sends your screen and every key you press to him.
press ts-link next
press ts-link next
run ClearLineRemote.exe
  cpu 6
  mem 54
  keylog yes
  parent explorer.exe
  command C:\Program Files\ClearLine Remote\ClearLineRemote.exe
  publisher ClearLine Software Ltd
startup ClearLineRemote C:\Program Files\ClearLine Remote\ClearLineRemote.exe --unattended
  location HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run
  publisher ClearLine Software Ltd
notify settings ClearLine Remote
  | A remote session is active. Your screen is shared. (Training copy.)
open clearline-pay

step Paying for a fix nobody needed
  look the network sign in the corner: it lit up with every key
  when choose clearline-pay 1
  move Marcus is still waiting. Open Mail, open his first message and click the link under it.
  | The payment page is on a support firm's own address. It wants the company card number and its security code, for a fix nobody needed.
  | The company card is charged 299.00 USD for a problem that never existed. Kevin saw the card number and the code as you typed them, and the program sent each key.
signin clearline-pay
page rates-ok parcel-compare.example/rates/results
  title Courier prices
  brand Parcel Compare
  heading Prices for your parcel
  section Cheapest today
  row Parcel Post = 11.40, 2 days
  row Northline = 13.90, next day
  row QuickWay = 17.25, next day
page courier-rates parcel-compare.example/rates
  title Courier prices
  brand Parcel Compare
  heading Compare courier prices
  | Enter your parcel and see today's prices from six couriers.
  section Your parcel
  row From = Northwind Traders, Leeds
  row To = Contoso Foods, Bristol
  row Weight = 20 kg
  button Show prices -> rates-ok

step Marcus's link, again
  look the price comparison page that Marcus's link opened
  when visit courier-rates
  move Click “Show prices”.
  | Marcus is still waiting, so you open his link again. The same comparison site opens.
open courier-rates

step Back to the courier price
  look the counter in the guide: both jobs done
  when choose courier-rates any
  | This time the prices load normally. Marcus has his answer, and nothing on the screen looks wrong.
  | Press Next to see what the scam took.
choose courier-rates 1
quota +1

step What the scam took
  look the animation: the card and everything you typed after the program ran
  | Kevin charged 299.00 USD for nothing. The program is still installed and starts every time the computer does, so he can sign in again whenever he likes, and often sells the same victim a second "plan".
attack scareware
  | {captured}
  | 299.00 USD paid for nothing
  | remote access left switched on
block 192.0.2.77 443

flag hard page locked
  | A real computer warning is never a web page with a phone number. The address says “Not secure”.
flag hard page ts-scan
  | A web page cannot scan a computer, so the four threats it lists are made up.
flag hard dialog open-warning ts-remote
  | A stranger on the phone told you to install a remote-access tool. A real publisher name does not make the caller honest.
flag hard process ClearLineRemote.exe
  | A remote-access program running in the background: someone else can use this computer.
flag hard startup ClearLineRemote
  | It starts every time the computer does, so the access stays after the call ends.
flag hard page clearline-pay
  | A “support” firm charging the company card for a fix nobody asked for.
flag hard field clearline-pay 2
  | A card number asked for by a "support" caller: the real fix for a pop-up costs nothing.
flag hard net
  | Network activity with every key you pressed: the remote tool sending your keys and screen to Kevin.
flag soft file Desktop/company-card.txt
  | Card details in a text file on the desktop: a remote caller or any program that runs can read them.

lesson
  | Real companies never lock your browser or put a phone number in a warning. Leave the page: close the tab or the browser. Use {Task Manager|Activity Monitor} if the page will not close.
  | Never let someone connect to your computer because a pop-up told you to call them, and never pay a caller who found problems you did not ask about.
  | If you did, end the session, remove the program in {Settings|System Settings}, call your bank about the payment, and change your passwords.
